00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.710
removed in 2022.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.730
And so, OK, hearing what Trump says, fine.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.719
But hearing from his followers and from Republican
donors, that's really the future of the party.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:56.530
And it's unfortunately as bad as anybody could
have imagined.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:02.090
We're going to look at an interview with a
woman that CNN spoke to in Florida at this

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:06.820
Republican donor event at which Donald Trump
also spoke over the weekend.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.939
I was looking at this like the Zapruder film.

00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:14.530
I mean, you you almost have to go frame by
frame because there is so much ignorance and

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:20.229
delusion and toxicity packed into a really
small amount of video.

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:23.700
So let's just jump right into the first clip.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.520
And I do believe that the election was stolen
and I do believe that it was a peaceful rally

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.899
that day and that just because people who
were in the Capitol were wearing Trump shirts

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:38.869
and Trump ads doesn't necessarily make them
Trump supporters.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:40.490
Anybody can get one of those shirts.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.490
So that is merely 18 seconds.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:49.790
And it is filled with ignorance that could
take months to overcome and change people's

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.249
minds about.

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:53.560
I do believe the election was stolen.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:02:00.409
She says she's not alone, but I mean, she's
a nut, but she's not a lone nut because 60

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:02.990
percent of Republicans believe exactly that.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:07.789
60 percent of Republicans believe that Donald
Trump was the rightful winner of the November

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:08.810
2012 election.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:14.319
Now, December, January, February, March, April
were now five months out.

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:15.879
Sixty percent of them still believe that.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:17.560
OK, that is not a fringe view.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:23.439
There are you could have tens of millions
of conversations just like this one with other

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:24.840
Trump voters.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.150
She says the Trump riots were mostly peaceful
and not really Trump supporters.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.650
Half of Republicans believe that.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.420
And the reporter tries to explain.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.920
We know that they were Trump supporters.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:43.630
There is no doubt when when we look at the
court cases, we find, you know, years of pro

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.980
trump social media activity.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.329
At some point, you have to apply Occam's razor.

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:53.880
Did they two years ago, three years ago, five
years ago, knowing on January six they were

00:02:53.880 --> 00:03:02.590
going to pretend to be Trump, let's start
falsely seeding SCD false pro Trump stuff

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:03.900
on their social media.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.239
Or if it walks like a duck and it sounds like
a duck, it's simply a duck.

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:10.469
And she doesn't care when she's presented
with that.

00:03:10.469 --> 00:03:11.469
Take a listen.

00:03:11.469 --> 00:03:15.950
But a lot of them have been shown to be real
Trump supporters, very true indictments that

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.769
they've been a long, long time support of
Trump.

00:03:19.769 --> 00:03:21.520
I don't know that to be true.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.599
So this is really a case of burden, of proof.

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:29.379
She believes without evidence that the Trump
is that the riots were fake.

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:30.379
Trump supporters.

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:32.120
There's no evidence of that.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.849
There is evidence that they are real Trump
supporters, but she hasn't seen it, so she

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:38.749
dismisses it out of hand.

00:03:38.749 --> 00:03:44.250
This is a really big epistemological hurdle
to clear, and we've talked about it before,

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.189
very, very difficult.

00:03:46.189 --> 00:03:50.659
And the most effective way is you build a
rapport with this person and over a period

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:53.849
of months, you can maybe get them to change
their view.

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:56.969
That doesn't work when it's 75 million people
that believe the stuff.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:00.970
It's just just a fact of math, quite frankly.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:01.970
Let's continue.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:06.480
I think some people who watch this who are
not Trump supporters and others say here's

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.450
a Trump supporter who's in denial about January
six.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:12.219
Would it not be better to sort of have to
say that to me?

00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:16.190
Because I've had friends and family already
say that to me, you know, because they disagree.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:21.540
They think that I'm crazy, that I'm a conspiracy
theorist because the election was stolen.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.600
Now, that is fascinating.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.210
She says forget about your audience saying
I'm in denial.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:32.139
My family and friends has already been telling
me that for months.

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:35.310
And yet it seems to only further her belief
that she is correct.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.340
This goes back to another principle we've
talked about.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.010
Oftentimes when people are, you know, kind
of bashed over the head with facts, they don't.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:49.710
They change their mind, they further attach
or align themselves to their beliefs when

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.160
they are told by others, that's crazy.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:59.740
They don't say, well, maybe it is, they further
plant their feet in those beliefs and and

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.979
and that it simply doesn't work, as we've
talked about before.

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:04.180
Let's look at more of this.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:09.570
Speaking of your family calling you conspiracy
theorists, Mergenthaler Green is going to

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.420
be speaking here this weekend.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:14.419
She's known as the some is that she went on.

00:05:14.419 --> 00:05:17.190
Congresswoman, I don't know what's so terrible,
terrible about Kuhnen.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.810
Can you tell me it's a faceless conspiracy
theory?

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:28.169
Well, let's hope you know what is so terrible
about conspiracy theories anyway.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:30.210
Can you tell me?

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.060
I mean, there's steroids.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.360
There are conspiracy theories behind JFK assassination.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.020
I'm old enough to remember all the conspiracy
theories that swirled around his assassination,

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:45.470
but it's always it's always painted in such
a negative way.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:52.009
These conspiracy theories are are cutting
into the foundation of American democracy

00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:55.300
or helping inspire a violent insurrection.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.569
No, I don't believe that's the case.

00:05:58.569 --> 00:06:02.990
And what way can you explain the delay the
election was stolen?

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.069
Well, see, that's where we part ways.

00:06:06.069 --> 00:06:08.440
I don't believe that it's a lie.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:15.040
I just love that you actually see if you're
watching how she seems to be back on the defensive

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.880
for a moment there.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.360
But then she comes up with something that
in some Republican circles is what I call

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.590
faux enlightened, a faux enlightened.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.330
You can always these are crutches.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:33.000
These are I wouldn't even call them epistemological
crutches because there's no epistemology there.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:40.340
It's a rhetorical crutch where you can always
say what's so bad about X anyway?

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:45.379
And that makes you, quote, open to quote ideas.

00:06:45.379 --> 00:06:50.290
This is a really common thing that people
on the right who know enough to know, they

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:55.599
know that they don't really have facts, but
they also have these rhetorical crutches they

00:06:55.599 --> 00:07:00.169
can put their weight on, like turning something
back on on the other person.

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:06.139
And then we get to what is now one of the
toughest problems that we're dealing with,

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:10.220
which is disagreements about what facts are.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:15.129
Now, I've made this distinction before, and
it's a really important one to understand.

00:07:15.129 --> 00:07:20.590
We're not experiencing a disagreement about
what the facts are.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.180
We've had that forever.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.440
We're having a problem of lots of people.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:29.979
Don't know what type of statement is a statement
of fact and what type of statement is a statement

00:07:29.979 --> 00:07:31.240
of opinion.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:35.569
Much of right wing media is built on the fact
that their audience doesn't know the difference.

00:07:35.569 --> 00:07:39.800
A huge portion of this country, particularly
on the American right, doesn't understand

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:41.840
that not all statements can be reduced to.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.400
We have a different set of equally valid beliefs.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.009
There are knowable facts and there are opinions.

00:07:48.009 --> 00:07:52.110
But tons of Americans don't know how to categorize
those types of statements.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.350
And there's really no easy recovery with that.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.490
And I know it's like a broken record at this
point.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.130
This goes back to education.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.490
This goes back to media literacy, critical
thinking stuff.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.560
You really have to start teaching around age
11 or 12.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.150
I'm not saying that folks like this woman
are, in a literal sense, too far gone.

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:13.300
But if you've listened to the phone calls
I got on the show, if you've seen the interviews

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:18.939
I do on the show, you know, the level of work
that would be required to change one mind

00:08:18.939 --> 00:08:24.599
for someone that is so indoctrinated like
this woman, imagine 75 million of these people.

00:08:24.599 --> 00:08:29.689
That's why I'm not optimistic that these folks
are the ones we can necessarily bring back

00:08:29.689 --> 00:08:30.689
in bulk.

00:08:30.689 --> 00:08:35.460
It seems we need to do a better job of educating
people never to fall for this stuff in the

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:36.460
first place.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:09:46.500
But it's going to be a very uphill battle.

